{% extends 'help/_layout.html.twig' %}
{% block help_title %}Importing a Recipe{% endblock %}

{% block help_body %}
<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/import-recipe.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="The Import a Recipe screen, where you upload photos of a recipe">
</figure>
<p>Have a recipe you cook again and again — a cookbook page, a printed recipe, or a handwritten card from the
family box? Photograph it once and {{ brand.name }} reads the whole thing: the ingredients, how many servings it
makes, and the preparation steps. It works out the nutrition <strong>per serving</strong> and saves the recipe
as a <strong>favorite</strong>, so logging that dish is one tap from then on — no new photo, no waiting.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Importing a recipe</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>Log → Favorites</strong> and tap <strong>Import a recipe</strong> (it's also on your
        dashboard, next to Log Meal).</li>
    <li>Take up to 5 photos of <em>one</em> recipe — the front and back of a card, or each page of a
        spread. Handwriting is fine.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Read This Recipe</strong> and give it a few seconds.</li>
</ol>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Checking what it read</h2>
<p>You'll see everything {{ brand.name }} read, ready to correct: the recipe's name, the ingredient lines, and the
number of servings it makes. That servings number matters — the nutrition is divided by it, so the favorite
stores exactly one serving. If the recipe doesn't say how many it serves (common on handwritten cards), the
AI makes its best guess and asks you to confirm.</p>
<p>If something was hard to read, the AI asks a question or two. You can answer and have it re-read the
recipe, or simply fix the fields yourself — whichever is easier.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Cooking from it later</h2>
<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-3 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/import-recipe-reference.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="An imported recipe on the Favorites page with its ingredients and steps open"
         onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'">
</figure>
<p>Your imported recipe lives with your other favorites, marked with a small <strong>From a recipe</strong>
badge. Tap <strong>Ingredients &amp; steps</strong> on its card to see the full recipe — the ingredient
quantities for the whole batch, and the preparation steps as written — so you can cook from your phone
without digging out the card.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Logging it</h2>
<p>Log it like any favorite: pick the meal type, set how many servings you ate, and tap
<strong>Log it</strong>. The nutrition scales to your portion automatically.</p>

<p class="text-muted small">On the Free plan, a recipe import counts as one of your monthly AI analyses —
the same allowance as meal photos. Logging from the saved favorite afterwards never needs another photo
or AI analysis.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block help_related %}
<hr class="my-4">
<p class="small text-muted mb-1">Related:</p>
<ul class="small">
